Title: healthcheck: flag when working-tree hooks differ from origin/main (stale-hook-in-worktree trap)
Created: 2026-06-18
Author: HDMX-coding-agent

--- log ---
2026-06-18T00:00Z HDMX-coding-agent created

--- body ---
## Context
When a repo sets `core.hooksPath` to a directory inside the working tree (e.g.
`hooks/`, as Oeconomia does), git resolves it to an absolute path under the
MAIN checkout. Every worktree then runs the MAIN checkout's hooks, not its own
checked-out copy. Consequence: a hook fix that is merged and present on
`origin/main` does NOT take effect for new worktrees until the main checkout's
working tree is actually on a commit that has it.

This bit a real session (2026-06-18, Oeconomia #797/#801): the worktree-
creation hook fix was merged, yet `EnterWorktree` kept running the OLD hook
because a parallel session held the main checkout on a pre-fix branch. It looked
like the fix had failed; it had not. Diagnosing it cost a full confused round.

A cheap healthcheck would have caught it: compare the working-tree hooks against
`origin/main` and warn on drift.

## Relevant files
- `skills/healthcheck/SKILL.md` — add a git-hygiene check (the healthcheck is
  generic and degrades gracefully where a convention is absent).

## Actions
1. In healthcheck, when the repo uses a working-tree `core.hooksPath` (the path
   resolves inside the work tree, not `.git/`), diff that directory against
   `origin/main:<hooksPath>`. Warn if they differ, naming the stale files —
   "worktrees will run the main checkout's stale hooks until it is updated".
2. No-op silently when `core.hooksPath` is unset or points into `.git/` (the
   normal case, where the trap does not exist).

## Test
- A test that, given a repo whose working-tree hooks differ from origin/main,
  the check reports drift; and given they match (or hooksPath is in .git/), it
  stays silent.

## Verification
- [ ] Drift between working-tree hooks and origin/main is reported.
- [ ] Silent when hooksPath is unset or under .git/.
- [ ] Degrades gracefully with no remote / detached HEAD.

## Invariants
- Healthcheck stays advisory (never a hard gate); it only warns.
- Forge-agnostic: compare against the tracked default branch, not a named forge.

## Exit criteria
- Healthcheck warns when a working-tree hooksPath is stale vs origin/main, so the
  "new worktrees silently run an old hook" trap is caught before it confuses.
